==English==

===Etymology===
Possibly from {{term|wiseacre}}.

The verb 'To Waz/Wazz' in northern English dialect means to urinate. In both West Yorkshire and East Lancashire, offal was very popular and when produced en masse the offal was cooked in linen socks that prevented the offal from being burnt and ruined when it came in to direct contact with the vessel in which it was being cooked. Unsurprisingly these socks took on the odour of the offal which is similar to urine. These linens were colloquially referred to as Waz Socks due to their pungent smell. The derogatory sense of a wazzock to this day refers to someone or thing of little purpose or worth.

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{context|British|mildly|pejorative|slang|lang=en}} a [[stupid]] or [[annoying]] person: specifically, an annoying [[know-it-all]].<ref>Maureen Dowd, "[http://www.nytimes.com/2012/07/29/opinion/sunday/dowd-mitts-olympic-meddle.html?_r=1 Mitt's Olympic Meddle]" (''New York Times'', July 28, 2012)</ref>

